            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Bread primarily symbolizes Jesus Christ (particularly his body in the Eucharist and his role as the Bread of Life) and God's physical and spiritual provision. 'The Word of God' is widely recognized as a prominent secondary or complementary symbolic meaning.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Bread symbolizes the word of God.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Jesus Christ, the Eucharist, and God's life-giving provision (which includes His Word).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The word of God.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book narrows the symbol exclusively to the Word of God, omitting the primary christological, sacramental, and physical provision meanings that dominate the consensus.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct contradiction, as the consensus explicitly recognizes 'the Word of God' as a valid and common meaning for bread; however, the book treats this alternative meaning as the sole referent.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The consensus identifies 'bread' as a multivalent symbol, primarily representing Christ but also commonly representing the Word of God based on texts like Deuteronomy 8:3 and Matthew 4:4. The book adopts this well-attested secondary meaning but refines the symbol by narrowing its scope entirely to this single referent.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The provided citations (Deuteronomy 8:3 and Matthew 4:4) contrast physical bread with the Word of God rather than explicitly identifying bread as a symbol for the Word. Furthermore, isolating this single meaning ignores the far more dominant New Testament identification of bread with Christ's body and the Eucharist.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
